  "query_preview": "..Rain gauge and radar data suggest a widespread area\n2 to 5 inches of rain (locally higher) has fallen since yesterday\nacross and south of the Mogollon Rim, with some stream gauges \nexceeding record stage heights. A mid-level trough has started to\npush off to the east and drier air follows in the wake of a \nfrontal passage, leaving a mostly dry and benign forecast for the\nrest of this week. River flooding may continue into at least\nWednesday, with one of the most notable impacts expected at\nWinslow. A record flood stage has been observed early this morning\non the Little Colorado at Winslow, with the levee expected to be\novertopped within the next several hours. \n\nLight snow accumulations were found mostly above 7500 feet tonight\nand freezing temperatures will make for slick spots this morning.\nAfter clearing this morning, mostly clear skies will dominate the\nforecast and aid in the start of drying amidst widespread\nflooding. Please visit or webpage for the latest flood warning\nproducts.",
